# Smithy specs and implementations stay in sync

When you change wire shapes, services, or persistence contracts, **update both sides** (or leave an explicit `@documentation` note in Smithy explaining intentional divergence).

## Pairings

| Spec package | Primary implementation(s) |
|--------------|---------------------------|
| [`packages/agent-identity-spec`](packages/agent-identity-spec) (`model/*.smithy`) | [`packages/agent-identity`](packages/agent-identity) — snapshot types in `src/snapshot/`, hashing, `ToolSpec`, identity, policy telemetry |
| [`packages/memories-spec`](packages/memories-spec) (`model/*.smithy`) | [`packages/memories-core`](packages/memories-core), [`packages/memories-persistence`](packages/memories-persistence) — rows, merge/search, adapter surfaces |

## What to do

1. **TS / runtime change** that affects interchange, hashes, or storage rows → update the matching Smithy shapes (namespace `cfd.agent_identity` or `cfd.memories`) and run validation.
2. **Smithy change** → align TypeScript types, public exports, and any persistence adapters that mirror those shapes.
3. After edits, run **`smithy validate model && smithy build`** from the relevant spec package (`bun run validate` if `package.json` defines it).

## Naming

- Prefer the same structure names and field names between Smithy and TS where they represent the same concept (`ToolSpecWire`, `IdentityLink`, `MergeMemoryParams`, etc.).
